WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.642 --> 00:00:07.475
If you wanna get around,

00:00:07.475 --> 00:00:09.889
if you wanna drive a car,
you wanna get on a plane,

00:00:09.889 --> 00:00:12.000
you need oil.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.993
It has been the foundation
of the 20th century economy.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.080
Once considered impossible,

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:26.233
American energy independence
has become a reality.

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:28.480
And for better or for worse,

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.140
it's all thanks to fracking.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.930
The spoils of which have spurred

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:34.230
what's commonly called

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:35.930
the Shale Revolution.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:38.140
It is almost impossible to overstate

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.420
the transformational
effect that fracking had

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:43.070
on US oil and natural gas production.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.260
It became this rallying cry

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.960
that the shale boom was
really gonna transform

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.030
the US into a dominant energy player.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.543
It changed everything really.

00:00:53.543 --> 00:00:56.090
The US went from producing
about five million barrels

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:57.940
of oil a day in 2008,

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:02.480
up to 13 million barrels of
oil just this last March.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:03.840
It was this, what we thought at the time,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.322
was gonna be this great
American success story.

00:01:06.322 --> 00:01:08.122
Except for one problem.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:11.443
The numbers never added up.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:16.440
For every dollar they brought in,

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.980
they'd be spending $1.10,
or $1.20, or $1.50.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.320
This has always been the
Achilles heel of the business.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.410
The oil sector always
needs to be very careful

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:29.860
about the level of investment it makes

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.000
and the cash returns to investors.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.010
The investment community did not finance

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:39.500
the shale revolution out of
the goodness of their hearts,

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.320
I think they liked the idea
of US energy independence,

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.950
but they wanted returns,
and for the most part,

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:47.370
they hadn't come.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.423
There has never been a better decade

00:01:49.423 --> 00:01:52.200
for growing US oil and gas production

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.000
and yet what was the worst
performing sector in the S&amp;P500?

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.040
Energy.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.280
That tells you a lot about this sector.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.130
26 US oil and gas producers

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.489
have filed for bankruptcy this year.

00:02:04.489 --> 00:02:06.570
This is the story of how shale

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:09.933
went from revolution to
a bottomless money pit.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:20.550
In order to understand how much shale

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:22.130
changed the energy game,

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.960
you have to go back a half century or so.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:25.960
For about 50 years,

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.940
America has been way short of oil and gas,

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:30.940
having to import these vital fossil fuels

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.930
that are essential to the
running of the economy.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:36.430
And everyone believed
that we would be importing

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:40.110
almost all our oil requirements
for the foreseeable future.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.720
And really that shaped American economics

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:43.930
for a decade.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.290
You had the Arab oil embargo in the 70's,

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:49.120
you've had wars, you've had recessions,

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.263
all driven by shortages of oil and gas.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.420
From 1970 until 2000,

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.190
the US imported most of its oil

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:00.073
from Saudi Arabia and Russia.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.980
Companies like Exxon and Chevron

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.060
were exploring drilling all over the world

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.513
in places like the Arctic, and Africa.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.893
But fracking changed everything.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:21.070
When we talk about fracking,

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:23.130
we basically talk about the combination

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:24.460
of two technologies,

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:27.950
horizontal drilling, and
hydraulic fracturing,

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:29.580
and when they come together,

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:32.690
it allows oil producers to get oil and gas

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:36.370
out of very, very dense shale rock,

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:37.950
very deep beneath the earth.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.620
And they really unlocked
huge, huge resources

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:42.743
of oil and gas.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:47.170
It was briefly profitable

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:49.054
as a gas game, but very quickly,

00:03:49.054 --> 00:03:51.640
the problem with any kind
of commodity industry

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:52.890
is supply and demand,

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.900
so natural gas fracking
unleashed such a plethora

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.180
of supply, price plummeted

00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.563
and it's never recovered.

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:02.270
After shooting itself in the foot

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:04.890
by flooding the gas market,
the fracking business

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.820
managed to pivot to oil.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.769
And what we find is that all
the lessons that were learned

00:04:09.769 --> 00:04:13.582
in natural gas could be
applied just as easily

00:04:13.582 --> 00:04:16.880
to deposits of oil,
because it's essentially

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:17.870
the same process.

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:20.450
A lot of people thought
this was hocus pocus,

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:23.640
it was never gonna work, but
there were a few companies

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.593
who decided to try it, just in case.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:28.583
Lo and behold, it worked.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.875
So, enormous amounts of
money flooded into the sector

00:04:34.875 --> 00:04:38.790
and you had lots of
companies took the money,

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:40.243
and went out drilling.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:43.665
It was a perfect storm.

00:04:43.665 --> 00:04:47.117
Near zero interest rates
prompted by the financial crisis,

00:04:47.117 --> 00:04:50.770
mixed with the seemingly
endless demand for oil and gas

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:54.570
at sky-high prices gave way
to a veritable feeding frenzy

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:56.700
of hungry investors on Wall Street.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:57.940
This made it very easy

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:01.190
for small, independent shale
companies to get started.

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:03.070
You could really just lease up land

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:04.820
and if you had partners, and you know,

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:07.530
even just a small amount of investment,

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:10.010
you could just try to figure
out where the good rock was.

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:12.470
And that meant that sometimes you lost

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:13.470
and sometimes you won,

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.830
but when you won, you won big.

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:18.350
For years, the big majors
didn't want anything

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.210
to do with it, they stayed out of it.

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:23.560
So it really was almost
like modern day wildcatting,

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.220
and people made fortunes.

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:30.990
Throughout American history,

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:33.649
oil has always attracted
the rebellious type.

00:05:33.649 --> 00:05:36.163
And fracking was no different.

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:40.711
Back then, and today, these
guys were called wildcatters.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:45.700
In the movies, you see those pictures of

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:50.640
the rugged Texas oil
wildcatter standing in front of

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.523
some spewing well.

00:05:54.867 --> 00:05:58.270
Wildcatter was basically someone who took

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:00.744
what was considered like
a small amount of money

00:06:00.744 --> 00:06:01.780
at the time,

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:05.010
and went out and leased
up a bunch of acreage

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:06.720
and poked holes in the ground

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.430
to try to figure out where the oil was.

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:11.450
And no one personified the modern day

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:14.653
shale wildcatter better
than Aubrey McClendon.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:19.860
We might have brought a way
for America and the world

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.230
to be able to increasingly say no to OPEC.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.550
Aubrey McClendon was the founder and CEO

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:29.000
of a company called Chesapeake Energy.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:32.790
He's a fascinating character,
just larger than life.

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:34.710
He was one of the first

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:38.920
to appreciate the scale of
American shale gas reserves.

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.529
If there's an old maxim
in the oil business,

00:06:41.529 --> 00:06:42.462
drill baby, drill!

00:06:42.462 --> 00:06:43.843
Drill baby, drill!

00:06:43.843 --> 00:06:45.640
Drill baby, drill!
Drill baby, drill!

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:47.170
That was Aubrey to a tee.

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:48.210
If you gave him money,

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:49.950
if he had access to money, any money,

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:52.470
no matter how hard he had
to scrounge to find it,

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:53.930
he was gonna go drill a well.

00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:58.780
With McClendon as shale's mascot,

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:01.690
companies like Chesapeake
and a slew of other startups

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:04.180
rode a wave of glory, and shale was hailed

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:06.800
as the next great American success story.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:10.510
So 2010 to 2014 were really
the glory days of shale.

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:12.840
This is when you had
everything going right.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.450
You had oil prices at $100 a barrel,

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:18.090
you had Wall Street throwing
money at these companies,

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:20.720
you had exploration being very successful,

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.490
finding all these new areas,

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:23.930
all these new basins.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:26.710
The idea that you could take something

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:28.510
that was in your own backyard

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:31.500
and make your country
this big global player

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.420
is really a romantic one,
and I think a lot of people

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:35.837
believed in that.

00:07:38.787 --> 00:07:40.670
And so the oil flowed.

00:07:40.670 --> 00:07:41.740
Lots of it.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:44.620
So much that America's
standing actually reversed

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:47.818
from a net importer to
a net exporter of oil.

00:07:47.818 --> 00:07:50.750
And for the wildcatters
and consumers alike,

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:52.743
it was a remarkable transformation.

00:07:52.743 --> 00:07:55.390
But what no one seemed to want to admit

00:07:55.390 --> 00:07:58.944
is that shale extraction
is incredibly expensive.

00:07:58.944 --> 00:08:01.260
And a shale well is very different

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:03.410
from a traditional oil well.

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:04.800
With a conventional well,

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:06.560
once you've drilled it,
if you're lucky enough

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:08.107
to hit natural gas, or oil,

00:08:08.107 --> 00:08:10.470
the well generally continues to produce

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:12.050
for a long, long time.

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:17.050
Shale wells by nature,
explode in this like spew

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:21.567
of production, and it's like
if you take a champagne bottle

00:08:21.567 --> 00:08:25.733
and you shake it, you're gonna
get bubbles flowing over.

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:29.530
But the decline that happens

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:31.700
after that initial burst of production,

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:35.110
you can see a shale well
lose 70 percent of production

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:36.300
after its first year.

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:38.364
I mean, it's pretty staggering.

00:08:38.364 --> 00:08:39.950
As a shale producer,
you're constantly fighting

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:41.770
this enormous decline rate.

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:44.700
You need to constantly be
drilling new wells, new wells,

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:47.160
new wells, new wells, which
is more money, more money,

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:48.070
more money.

00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:51.490
If you think about the
mindset of dot com companies

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:54.070
back in the day, it was get market share,

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:56.060
just grow, grow, grow, claim the market,

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:57.660
then it's all yours,
then you're gonna be able

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:58.910
to produce profits.

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:01.520
And Aubrey McClendon's mindset
and the mindset of many

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:03.773
in the shale industry
was much the same way.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:08.240
And while investors kept
pouring in new money,

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:11.280
shale executives were
raking in huge salaries,

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:13.610
even as their companies were sinking.

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:16.220
Executives were paid based
on production growth,

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:20.280
not on profits, and if
you reward an executive

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:24.110
to grow, then grow is
what they're going to do.

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:26.810
Tell an oil guy that not
only does he have money

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:28.640
to go drill, but you're gonna pay him

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:30.700
if he can go and produce more oil,

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:33.860
he's gonna go produce all
the oil he possibly can.

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:37.650
You can't slow down, or
your company shrinks,

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:39.200
but if you keep growing,

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:41.640
you're just constantly
putting cash in the ground

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:42.810
to drill new wells

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.417
and eventually investors are gonna say,

00:09:45.417 --> 00:09:47.927
"Wait a second, I need
you to stop drilling wells

00:09:47.927 --> 00:09:50.420
"if you're not gonna be able
to give me my money back."

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:51.720
Over the last decade,

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.610
an estimated $340 billion dollars

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:56.760
has been spent on US fracking.

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:59.600
It's what everyone refers
to as the treadmill.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:02.900
The problem, of course, is
when the treadmill stops,

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:05.483
or when you are forced
to get off the treadmill.

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:10.683
How low could oil go?

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:12.633
It's been referred to

00:10:12.633 --> 00:10:14.363
as the Thanksgiving Day massacre.

00:10:15.934 --> 00:10:18.940
There is going concern
amongst some members of OPEC

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:21.880
that shale was eating their lunch.

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:25.250
Because you had this enormous
growth in the sector.

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:27.510
Saudi Arabia, that is
taking the bigger picture,

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:30.310
to protect the oil price,
we have to not cut,

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:33.620
because otherwise shale
oil will get the advantage.

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:35.210
The entire shale business model

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:38.140
was predicated on high oil prices.

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:41.370
Any serious drop and it
all comes crashing down.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:42.203
Who's winning?

00:10:42.203 --> 00:10:43.070
Consumers.

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:43.903
Who's losing?

00:10:43.903 --> 00:10:46.390
Well obviously producers,
but maybe people who've lent

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:48.960
to producers need to be
a little bit more worried

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:50.330
than perhaps they have been.

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:52.560
US frackers who had been promising

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:54.410
they could make money,
suddenly it was clear

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:56.400
they couldn't make money.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.180
And the capital going into the
industry started to dry up.

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:03.130
While over 100 companies
ended up declaring bankruptcy.

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:06.060
And people said the
shale revolution is over.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:08.020
But since when has a slew of bankruptcies

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:11.270
and a highly volatile market
stopped any self-respecting

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:12.330
entrepreneur?

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:13.970
There was still a lot of people willing

00:11:13.970 --> 00:11:16.143
to invest billions of dollars in shale.

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:19.200
A big reason why investors
remained interested

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.490
in shale was the rediscovery
of a huge reserve in Texas

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:24.270
called the Permian Basin.

00:11:24.270 --> 00:11:27.800
The hype around this region
became known as Permania.

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:30.710
And so even though
there was this downturn,

00:11:30.710 --> 00:11:32.963
the music really didn't stop playing.

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:36.260
Guys like Aubrey McClendon

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:39.530
used permania to continually
sell the shale story,

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:42.000
even as the price per
barrel made the model

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:43.860
totally unsustainable.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:45.800
And fighting back the cloud over shale

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:47.830
was gonna prove as futile
as trying to change

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:49.860
the direction of the wind.

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:53.443
The reckoning came in 2016,
with McClendon's death.

00:11:56.290 --> 00:11:58.000
The Oklahoma City Police
Department responded

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:01.970
to a fatality accident
involving Mr. Aubrey McClendon.

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:03.710
The government had been investigating him

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:05.030
on price fixing charges,

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:08.710
and they indicted him
in the spring of 2016,

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:10.570
and the day after he was indicted,

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:14.970
he went out in his SUV and
hit a bridge at top speed,

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:16.410
and was killed.

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:18.760
His death was almost
like the punctuation mark

00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:21.107
underscoring what was supposedly the end

00:12:21.107 --> 00:12:24.105
of the shale revolution, but it came back!

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:27.360
And the main reason it came back

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:30.040
is because we were still in
this super low interest rate

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:30.880
environment.

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:34.110
Investors didn't have anywhere
else to put their money,

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:36.260
and so it came roaring back.

00:12:36.260 --> 00:12:38.350
Oil prices continued to rise steadily,

00:12:38.350 --> 00:12:40.800
and shale execs drilled harder than ever,

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:42.630
praying for increasing oil prices

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:45.200
and promising returns
to anxious investors.

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:47.157
2020 was supposed to be the year

00:12:47.157 --> 00:12:52.157
where the top 20, 25 players
in the independent US

00:12:52.265 --> 00:12:55.810
oil industry were
supposed to, in aggregate,

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:59.050
turn cash flow positive
for the first time.

00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:01.450
But, of course, that didn't happen.

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:05.870
In March of 2020, Russia
triggered an oil war

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:08.010
with Saudi Arabia, effectively killing

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:11.870
an important OPEC deal,
cratering global oil prices.

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:13.306
And as if that wasn't enough.

00:13:13.306 --> 00:13:15.910
The coronavirus pandemic
is triggering fears

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:17.510
of a global recession.

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:20.600
Crude oil drops below $30 a barrel.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:24.600
The impact on the oil is disproportionate.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:29.350
Oil prices crashed,
and everything changed.

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:31.820
Within weeks, you started
seeing the impacts

00:13:31.820 --> 00:13:35.201
of the coronavirus and
the subsequent lockdowns

00:13:35.201 --> 00:13:37.800
having effect on oil demand.

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:39.910
And unlike the last downturn,

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:44.320
this one was really triggered
by a complete collapse

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:45.410
in demand.

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:47.780
Global demand for oil was already low,

00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:50.480
since governments imposed
widespread lockdowns,

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:53.870
and airlines grounded flights
because of the disease.

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:55.500
There were lots of passive investors,

00:13:55.500 --> 00:13:58.703
people who own oil futures,
but have zero interest

00:13:58.703 --> 00:14:02.720
in taking delivery of a
physical barrel of crude oil

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:05.620
suddenly realizing that they
were gonna be on the hook

00:14:05.620 --> 00:14:08.340
for taking delivery of crude oil.

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:11.630
I think what everyone will
remember for many years to come

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:16.630
is the day oil prices fell
to negative $37 a barrel.

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:19.933
Since then, oil prices have bounced back,

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:24.240
but the shale wildcatters have not.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:26.993
Some of the quickest to
rise have already fallen,

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:30.950
including McClendon's white
knight, Chesapeake Energy,

00:14:30.950 --> 00:14:34.000
which filed for bankruptcy
in June of 2020.

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:36.330
Are we almost through
this bankruptcy cycle

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:38.710
in the distressed oil and gas patch?

00:14:38.710 --> 00:14:39.720
We are not.

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:43.520
For a lot of these guys, it
was going to happen anyway,

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:46.502
and this really just accelerated it.

00:14:46.502 --> 00:14:48.370
The tally is still coming in,

00:14:48.370 --> 00:14:49.930
but the number of jobs lost

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:51.600
due to the collapse in the shale business

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:53.520
is going to be crushing.

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:56.050
Fracking actually provided
some four million jobs

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:58.760
in the decade since the financial crisis.

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:00.060
It's a sudden demise,

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:02.029
perhaps a hint of things to
come, for the beleaguered

00:15:02.029 --> 00:15:04.760
shale oil industry.

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:06.250
Yet amidst this collapse,

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:09.520
shale execs have been
taking home huge bonuses.

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:13.230
Oil and gas executives have
typically made well over

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:15.790
100 percent of their target bonuses,

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:18.980
even as their returns or stock performance

00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:21.101
has been in the negatives.

00:15:21.101 --> 00:15:22.790
Ban fracking now!

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:24.500
And now that the bubble has burst,

00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:27.200
many environmentalists have
seized on these failures

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:29.500
to make their case against shale.

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:31.790
You actually have a
coming together, almost,

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:34.100
of environmentalist and investors to say

00:15:34.100 --> 00:15:36.180
just keep the oil in the ground,

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:39.070
because there is too much of it.

00:15:39.070 --> 00:15:40.931
Environmentalists were delighted

00:15:40.931 --> 00:15:44.730
by any reports that fracking
wasn't financially viable

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:47.810
because it appeared to be
support for their claim

00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:50.270
that this industry needed to go away.

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:53.130
The lack of profits in
the fracking industry

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:55.340
means that it's all of us, it's taxpayers

00:15:55.340 --> 00:15:57.440
who are gonna be stuck
with the cleanup cost

00:15:57.440 --> 00:15:59.070
with what it's done to our water supply,

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:01.243
with what it's done to our environment.

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:08.410
The lure of American dominance

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:10.505
in the energy sector remains strong

00:16:10.505 --> 00:16:13.670
and despite the shale revolution's
faulty business model,

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:16.040
and unresolved environmental concerns,

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:18.770
the powerful desire to
extract oceans of oil

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:22.780
that lay beneath our feet
will likely never relent.

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:25.838
Fracking has come back from
the dead several times.

00:16:25.838 --> 00:16:29.030
I wouldn't count the industry out,

00:16:29.030 --> 00:16:31.580
I don't know that we'll
ever see the heyday

00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:34.350
that we did in the last few years again,

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:35.550
but I think it's too soon to say

00:16:35.550 --> 00:16:37.023
that fracking is going away.

